# modules.cpp file system interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other specifications. This document specifies mm.fs, the
portable file interface; mm.fs.conformance, the checks every volume is held
to; the mount interfaces mm.fs.local and mm.fs.native; and platform.linux.fs,
their Linux provider; mm.fs.littlefs and platform.pico.fs.littlefs, littlefs
on Pico; mm.fs.fat and platform.pico.fs.fat, FAT on Pico. All exist and are
tested; mm.fs and mm.fs.conformance build for every target. What remains is
planned in drafts/plan-mm-fs-5.mdy, which this document does not make
official.

# What mm.fs is

One API for every volume. A program opens, reads, writes, and lists files
through mm.fs; a driver implements Volume; a device implements BlockDevice
or FlashDevice. mm.fs holds no driver, is not a platform-interface, and needs
no provider. It allocates nothing and serves one caller at a time: its path
buffers and mount table are static.

# Paths

A path is absolute, '/'-separated UTF-8. normalize makes it canonical before
any driver sees it: repeated '/' collapse, "." drops, ".." removes the
previous component and never climbs above "/", a trailing '/' is ignored.
The result is "/" or "/" followed by components joined by single '/'.

- An empty path, one not starting with '/', or one holding a NUL is
  BadArgument.
- A component over max_name (255) bytes, or a result over max_path (255)
  bytes or the caller's buffer, is NameTooLong. The path is resolved left to
  right, so it must fit at every step.
- out and length change only on Ok.

A driver therefore receives a volume-relative path with no leading '/', no
"." or "..", and "" for its root, and cannot be handed a way out of its
volume.

Names are case-preserving; whether two spellings name one file is the
volume's, and mm.fs does not hide the difference.

# The mount table

mount(prefix, volume) attaches a volume the caller owns at "/" or "/"
followed by one component. Another shape is BadArgument; a mounted prefix is
Exists; a volume already mounted elsewhere is Busy; more than max_mounts (8)
is TooMany. unmount(prefix) asks the volume to flush, returns a failed flush
and keeps the volume mounted, is Busy while any File or Directory on it is
open, and is NotFound for a prefix not mounted.

Every path call resolves its normalised path to the longest mounted prefix.
Prefixes are one component deep, so at most one non-root mount matches, and
it beats "/". "/usbstick" is not under "/usb". A path under no mount is
NotFound.

mm.fs keeps three rules itself, the same on every volume: rename between
mounts is CrossVolume; a mount point exists (make_directory is Exists) and
cannot be removed or renamed (BadArgument); a File's access is checked
before the driver is asked.

# Files and directories

File and Directory are move-only values holding a mount slot, the driver's
handle, and for a File its access. Their destructors close, ignoring the
result; a program that cares about a failed flush calls close. close leaves
the value closed whatever it returns, because the driver frees its handle
either way.

- open and open_directory into a value that is already open are
  BadArgument: mm.fs does not close it silently.
- Every call on a closed value is BadArgument.
- read on a file opened Write or Append, and write or truncate on one opened
  Read, are BadArgument. seek past the end of a file opened Read is
  BadArgument; on a writable file it is allowed, and the next write extends
  the file with zeros.
- read's count short of the request, including zero, is end of file.
  write's count short of the request comes with NoSpace, and the count is
  set then too; otherwise counts and offsets change only on Ok.
- A write is durable after sync or close; what durable means is the
  volume's.
- Directory::next returns entries without "." or "..", in the volume's
  order, with done set once they are exhausted. A name buffer too small is
  NameTooLong and the entry is not consumed; max_name bytes always suffice.

stat, make_directory (the parent must exist), remove (a file, or an empty
directory), rename (never replacing a target: Exists), and space take paths.
mounted(prefix, volume) answers the volume mounted at a prefix, which is how
a mount interface finds what it attached.

# The clock

set_clock installs a function pointer answering seconds since 1970-01-01,
and now() is what a driver stamps: the clock's answer, or 0. mm.fs names no
clock module, so a program without a clock pulls in none.

# The driver seams

Volume has one virtual per operation, keyed by the driver's handles, every
one answering Unsupported by default except flush, which answers Ok. The
rules a driver keeps beyond what mm.fs enforces are written on Volume:

- a file open for writing cannot be opened again, removed, or renamed: Busy;
- a file open for reading can be opened again for reading;
- rename never replaces an existing target: Exists;
- close frees the handle even when it fails.

BlockDevice is fixed-size blocks rewritten in place, which FAT wants;
FlashDevice is raw flash -- read anywhere, program only erased bytes, erase
whole blocks -- which littlefs wants. They are different promises and mm.fs
keeps both. McuStorage is mm.mcu's block storage as a BlockDevice, polling
the device on every call and answering TransportError while none is ready;
McuFlash is mm.mcu's flash region as a FlashDevice: its geometry in erase
blocks, and reads, programs, and erases passed through. These two are the
devices mm.fs supplies, and the reason it uses mm.mcu.

# Mount interfaces

A driver's volume reaches a program through a mount interface, a
platform-interface module whose provider the selected SDK or board binds:
a portable surface, a Provider seam, and an Unsupported fallback. A program
that reaches an interface its lane binds no provider for is unavailable on
that lane, as with every platform-interface. Each interface's mount asks the
provider to attach a volume and publishes it with mm::fs::mount, detaching it
again if publishing fails; its unmount refuses with BadArgument a volume it
did not attach, so a volume mounted at the same prefix by other means is
never handed to its provider.

- mm.fs.local, mount(prefix, options): the board's own storage, whatever the
  platform calls that. Options carry read_only and format_if_blank, the
  latter for platforms whose storage can be blank.
- mm.fs.littlefs, mount(prefix, device, options), unmount(prefix), and
  format(device): littlefs on a FlashDevice. mount is Corrupt for a device
  holding no littlefs volume and erases nothing; format writes an empty
  volume over the whole device. Options carry read_only and block_cycles.
- mm.fs.fat, mount(prefix, device, options), unmount(prefix), and
  format(device, format): FAT12, FAT16, and FAT32 on a BlockDevice of
  512-byte blocks. mount is Corrupt for a device holding no FAT volume and
  writes nothing to it. format writes an empty volume over the whole device
  in an MBR partition, as a PC expects; Format::Fat is FAT12 or FAT16 as
  FAT's rules choose for the size, Format::Fat32 FAT32, Format::Automatic
  either. Options carry read_only.
- mm.fs.native, mount(prefix, root, options): an absolute directory of the
  platform's own file system. A relative root is BadArgument, a missing one
  NotFound, a file NotDirectory. With contain_symlinks, on by default, a
  symbolic link whose target lies outside the root is treated as absent.

Opening fence
SDK            mm.fs.local                mm.fs.native       mm.fs.littlefs             mm.fs.fat
linux-x86_64   platform.linux.fs          platform.linux.fs  (none)                     (none)
linux-aarch64  platform.linux.fs          platform.linux.fs  (none)                     (none)
pico-arm       platform.pico.fs.littlefs  (none)             platform.pico.fs.littlefs  platform.pico.fs.fat
pico-riscv     platform.pico.fs.littlefs  (none)             platform.pico.fs.littlefs  platform.pico.fs.fat
Closing fence

# The Linux provider

platform.linux.fs serves both interfaces through the C++ standard library
alone: std::filesystem, with its std::error_code overloads, for the tree,
and std::filebuf for the bytes. Four volumes may be attached at once, each
with 32 open files and 8 open directories.

- mm.fs.local mounts the device map's directory.0, read-only unless
  directory.0.writable is yes, or, when the map names no directory, the
  working directory, writable. A map that failed to resolve is BadArgument,
  as for every Linux provider.
- Opening decides from status() first, because std::filebuf before C++23
  has no no-replace mode: CreateNew checks for the file, and a file that
  must be made or emptied is made by a separate truncating open. Another
  process could create the file between the two; mm.fs's one-caller promise
  covers this program, not the host.
- A file open for both reading and writing is repositioned between a write
  and a read, as a C stream must be.
- rename checks the target first, because std::filesystem::rename replaces
  it; remove and rename of anything open beneath the path is Busy; a file
  open for writing cannot be opened again.
- Offsets are std::streamoff, sixty-four bits in libstdc++ on every Linux
  lane, the 32-bit Arm one included.
- modified is last_write_time converted with std::chrono::clock_cast; the
  mm.fs clock is not used, because the kernel stamps files itself.
  read_only is the volume's flag or a missing owner write permission.
- sync is std::filebuf::pubsync: the bytes reach the kernel. The standard
  library has no fsync, so this provider does not promise they survive a
  power cut.
- Errors map from std::errc in one table: no_such_file_or_directory
  NotFound, file_exists Exists, not_a_directory NotDirectory, is_a_directory
  IsDirectory, directory_not_empty NotEmpty, no_space_on_device NoSpace,
  read_only_file_system, permission_denied, and operation_not_permitted
  ReadOnly, filename_too_long NameTooLong, too_many_files_open and
  too_many_files_open_in_system TooMany, device_or_resource_busy Busy,
  cross_device_link CrossVolume, anything else TransportError. A failed
  stream call reports through errno, as the other Linux providers read it.
- Names are case-sensitive bytes, and other processes may change the
  directory under the volume.

# littlefs on Pico

platform.pico.fs.littlefs serves mm.fs.littlefs and mm.fs.local on every Pico
board. mm.fs.local mounts littlefs on McuFlash, mm.mcu's flash region at the
top of program flash, which a program flashed over UF2 leaves intact; with
format_if_blank, the default, a region whose first two erase blocks are all
0xFF -- littlefs's superblock pair never written -- is formatted, and any
other region that does not mount is Corrupt and left as it is.

littlefs v2.11.3 is vendored by platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/littlefs/vendor.sh
at commit 6cb4e86540eca0d9ba62500a298385c9d863c8be into a git-ignored
upstream/, with its BSD-3-Clause licence committed beside it, and compiled by
the Pico bridge only for a program that links the provider;
docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy says how.

The provider follows platform.pico.mcu's pattern. littlefs/adapter.c, C,
includes littlefs and owns its state in static pools -- two volumes, eight
open files each with its own 256-byte cache, four open directories -- behind
a private ABI, littlefs/lfs-c.h; the C++ module includes only that header,
through littlefs/lfs-cxx.h, maps results and paths, and adapts a FlashDevice
to the adapter's device record through C-linkage callbacks.

The adapter keeps the Volume rules in C:

- Each disposition is decided from lfs_stat first. littlefs cannot create or
  truncate through a read-only open, so a Read open that must do either
  makes the file with a writable open and close first.
- A file open for writing cannot be opened again, removed, or renamed; an
  open directory cannot be removed or renamed; lfs_rename's replacing of a
  target is refused as Exists; a directory cannot be moved into itself.
- Directory listings leave out the "." and ".." littlefs returns, and an
  entry whose name does not fit stays pending for the next call.
- littlefs records no times. The adapter keeps each file's modification time
  in custom attribute 0x74, eight bytes of seconds from the mm.fs clock,
  which littlefs writes atomically with the file's data on sync and close.
  littlefs reads attributes on open only with read access, so the adapter
  loads the stored time before every open: a file opened for writing and
  closed unchanged keeps its time.
- littlefs's configuration: one erase block is a littlefs block, the caches
  are one program unit (256 bytes on Pico, at most 256), the lookahead is 32
  bytes, block_cycles comes from the options (500 for mm.fs.local), and
  littlefs is built with LFS_NO_MALLOC, LFS_NO_DEBUG, LFS_NO_WARN, and
  LFS_NO_ERROR.
- Errors map from littlefs's: NOENT NotFound, EXIST Exists, NOTDIR
  NotDirectory, ISDIR IsDirectory, NOTEMPTY NotEmpty, NOSPC and FBIG NoSpace,
  NAMETOOLONG NameTooLong, NOMEM TooMany, CORRUPT Corrupt, BADF and INVAL
  BadArgument. An I/O error the device explained is the device's own answer,
  so a flash timeout is Timeout.

The tracked platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/littlefs/test/harness.c provides 77
native checks through the ABI. It is compiled with adapter.c and the pinned
littlefs using the host C compiler, against a RAM NOR flash that refuses to
program a byte that is not erased. The checks cover: the
dispositions, append, extension, truncate, timestamps kept and updated,
listing and its retry, the Busy rules, rename, the file pool, NoSpace,
remounting, read-only mounts, a device timeout, and a damaged volume left
unerased. The module build compiles no C, so test.sh does not run it.

# FAT on Pico

platform.pico.fs.fat serves mm.fs.fat on every Pico board, over FatFs R0.16:
on a host-port board, FAT on the USB drive through McuStorage. It follows
platform.pico.fs.littlefs's shape: fatfs/adapter.c, C, includes FatFs,
supplies its disk I/O layer and get_fattime, and owns its state in static
pools -- two FATFS work areas, eight open files, four open directories --
behind a private ABI, fatfs/fat-c.h, which the C++ module reaches through
fatfs/fat-cxx.h.

FatFs is vendored by platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/fatfs/vendor.sh: ChaN's
ff16.zip, refused unless its SHA-256 is
99f7dc1f7e095356e4a9e3dbe29959090d8b948afe2bbc5441e52fdf4b85449e, unpacked
into a git-ignored upstream/, or taken from MM_FATFS_ARCHIVE; its licence is
committed beside it. The configuration is fatfs/ffconf.h, upstream's with
these values changed and passed to every FatFs translation unit with
-include, so upstream's copy is never read: FF_USE_MKFS 1, FF_CODE_PAGE 437
(upstream's 932 carries far larger tables), FF_USE_LFN 1 with UTF-8 names
(FF_LFN_UNICODE 2), FF_VOLUMES 2, FF_FS_TINY 1, FF_FS_LOCK 12, and
FF_NORTC_YEAR 2026. exFAT stays off: a commercial product needs a licence
from Microsoft for it.

The adapter keeps the Volume rules FatFs does not keep by itself:

- Each disposition is decided from f_stat first; a Read open that must make
  or empty its file does so with a writable open first.
- A file open for writing cannot be opened again, removed, or renamed, and an
  open directory cannot be removed or renamed. FatFs's lock table,
  FF_FS_LOCK, refuses the same under another spelling of the name, since FAT
  matches names without regard to case.
- rename refuses an existing target and a directory moving into itself.
- Every Append write seeks to the end first; FatFs's FA_OPEN_APPEND moves to
  the end only at open.
- A seek past the end is remembered rather than applied, because f_lseek
  would extend the file with whatever the clusters held; the next write fills
  the gap with zeros, reads in between return nothing, and tell reports the
  remembered offset.
- The root has a stat, which f_stat refuses to give, and directory listings
  leave out "." and ".." should FatFs ever return them.
- Timestamps are FAT's local date and time read as UTC, two-second
  resolution, from the mm.fs clock through get_fattime, or 1 January 2026
  with no clock.
- A device must have 512-byte blocks, FF_MAX_SS; formatting borrows a free
  FatFs drive for the length of f_mkfs.
- FR_DENIED is read by what was being done: NoSpace when making something,
  NotEmpty when removing a directory, ReadOnly otherwise. FR_DISK_ERR is the
  device's own answer when it gave one. The rest map one for one: FR_NO_FILE
  and FR_NO_PATH NotFound, FR_EXIST Exists, FR_NO_FILESYSTEM and FR_INT_ERR
  Corrupt, FR_LOCKED Busy, FR_TOO_MANY_OPEN_FILES TooMany, FR_TIMEOUT
  Timeout, the invalid-argument results BadArgument.

The tracked platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/fatfs/test/harness.c provides 84
native checks. It is compiled with adapter.c, the pinned FatFs and ffconf.h
using the host C compiler, against a RAM block device filled with 0xA5 so a
byte nothing wrote cannot pass for zero. The checks cover: the dispositions, appends after
seeks, a zero-filled gap, truncate, timestamps, long names, listing and its
retry, the Busy rules including a name under another case, rename, the file
pool, NoSpace with a short count, remounting, read-only mounts, a device
timeout, an unformatted device left unwritten, and FAT and FAT32 formats.
Dropping the gap fill, or FatFs's lock table, makes it fail.

apps/fat-smoke waits up to ten seconds for the board's block storage, mounts
its FAT volume at "/usb", runs the conformance checks, and unmounts; it never
formats, and skips without storage or a drive. scripts/build-fat.sh builds
it, by default for pico2_usb_host.

# Conformance

mm.fs.conformance::run(prefix, report) runs the contract as 25 checks inside
a scratch directory, mm-fs-conformance, beneath the prefix, removing it
before and after. It covers the four dispositions, append after a seek, short
reads, extension past the end, truncate, sync, stat, access refusals, seek
past the end of a read-only file, the IsDirectory and NotDirectory
refusals, directory listing and its NameTooLong retry, make_directory,
remove, rename, the Busy rules, sharing between readers, paths through "."
and "..", space, and calls on a closed File. It leaves to the volume what the
contract does: case sensitivity, timestamps, entry order. run answers Ok when
the checks could run and reports passes, failures, and the first failure's
description; when the scratch directory cannot be made it returns that
status and leaves the report untouched.

Every driver is meant to pass it, on the host or in a target smoke
application, which is what the same API everywhere means in practice.

apps/fs-smoke mounts mm.fs.local at "/data", runs the checks there, prints
the counts and the first failure, and unmounts; scripts/build-fs.sh builds it,
and on Linux runs it with --run. It skips, exiting 0, where mm.fs.local
answers Unsupported or NotFound.

# Tests

tests/mm/fs exercises normalize and its limits, mount shapes and slots,
resolution, mm.fs's own rules, a failed flush, moves and destructors, the
clock, NoSpace, and McuStorage on an unserved platform, and runs the
conformance checks over an in-memory reference volume at "/" and under a
prefix, and the mount interfaces against their fallbacks and recording
providers. Breaking one rule of the reference volume -- letting rename
replace its target -- makes the conformance test fail.

tests/mm/linux runs the same checks over platform.linux.fs on a temporary
directory, and checks that files are the host's files both ways, that
symlinks stay inside the root, that a read-only volume refuses every write,
that roots are checked, that mm.fs.local mounts the working directory, the
directory.N map keys, and the errc table. Dropping the provider's rename
target check makes the Linux conformance run fail on exactly that check.
